Z||000000||oreif_GT87||09-26-2000||09:47 AM||||I cant get a 283 for free from a 64 chevy pick-up does any know about how much HP this motor would have. It is stock ...not sure if it came from the pickup or not. <P>Thanks,<P>Black GT87||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||09-26-2000||01:45 PM||||i've driven a rather stock 283 only mods (performer intake and holley 600vacuum) in a 63 Chevy truck to 14.8 in the 1/4.. they are SUPER patient motors.. they work all the time and have pretty decent torque..  this motor mated to a Muncie "rock crusher" 4spd. on the dyno lays down 195hp and 245lbft. at the wheels... of a NEVER toucher 63 Chevy Stepside (87k miles). that would make a great motor for a swap... decent on fuel and Plenty of torque for tire churnin! <P>oh welcome to the forum!<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 09-26-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||oreif_GT87||09-26-2000||02:12 PM||||hey thanks for the reply, my father is giving me the motor..and im ordering a holley intake, carb and a new cam for it.<BR>So it should be pretty stout...just need some info about what type of flywheel i should use...i have a 5 speed manual .<P>Thanks <P>87 GT||||reg||14
Z||000003||mrfixit58||09-26-2000||02:24 PM||||I've owned two vehicles with 283s. Nice motor which responds well to modifications. The trick to making good usuable power is to get a good set of heads and DON'T over carb. <P>On my last one (I had it installed into a 1970 Chev LUV truck) I just had a set of plain stock heads with a Holley "Z" manifold and a 450 cfm carb. A mild performance cam (I don't remember the numbers) and stock cast iron rams horn exhaust manifolds. I used to out run early 1980's Camaros and Firebirds without downshifting (power-to-weight ratio is the ticket  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ).<P>You won't make the power that a 350 will but you can't argue with FREE!<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000004||oreif_GT87||09-26-2000||02:37 PM||||Thanks for the info ..i was going to go for 600 cfm carb...but do you think thats too much.?<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000005||Cooter||09-26-2000||04:34 PM||||600 might be a bit much unless it is a vacuum secondary carb- then it will only open as much as the engine can use unless you get goofy with the adjustment springs. I have nothing against a 283 (I had a 327 in a Camaro that was really strong), but if I were going to have a small block, I'd rather have a higher displacement because they are going to weigh the same. But then again, it is hard to beat a free engine!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Archie||09-26-2000||09:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by oreif_GT87:<BR><B>I cant get a 283 for free from a 64 chevy <P>Thanks,<P>Black GT87</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Please note: My V-8 kit and all copies of the Zumwalt kit. (Including the copies by Shelby, Fiero Plus, High-tech Developement & others) all mount the Alternator to the forward end of the left (forward) cylinder head. These alternator mounts use holes that GM drilled into the end of the cylinder head. GM started drilling these holes into the end of the cylinder head in about 1968. Most cylinder heads made prior to 1968 don't have these holes in them. Thus I quote the following line from my web site "Engines that can be used include any small block Chevy from 1968 to present,.." You can find the whole application statement at <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/v8fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/v8fiero.htm</A>  . Others won't tell you this (maybe they don't know) but I will.<P>If you decide to not use the A/C compressor you can mount the Alternator down where the A/C compressor normally mounts.<P>Otherwise be informed that this must be a consideration for your 283.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfixit58||09-27-2000||07:41 AM||||Good point Archie. I'd almost forgotton about that. One other thing to consider: if you decide to upgrade the heads to 68 or later models, avoid the 305 heads. The steam holes around the bottom of the heads are very close to the edge. This can lead to coolant leaking into either the cylindar and/or oil or down the side of the block. I learned this the hard way... after I paid to have a three angle valve job  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> .<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000008||oreif_GT87||09-27-2000||07:55 AM||||Thanks,Archie I didnt realize that...i guess i will have to replace the heads..Thanks for the info<P>87GT||||reg||1
